Does self-distillation improve reasoning? Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more × Back to arXiv License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2610.18613v1 [cs.LG] 13 Oct 2026 \__cmd_normalize_type_g:w g \__cmd_normalize_type_g:w g \__cmd_normalize_type_g:w g \__cmd_normalize_type_g:w g \__cmd_normalize_type_g:w g \__cmd_normalize_type_g:w g Does self-distillation improve reasoning? Jason Ken Adhinarta † † thanks: Equal contribution. Affiliation: Massachusetts Institute of Technology Raina Wu 1 1 footnotemark: 1 Affiliation: Massachusetts Institute of Technology Ashutosh Sharma Affiliation: MIT-IBM Computing Research Lab Avinash Balakrishnan Affiliation: MIT-IBM Computing Research Lab Rameswar Panda Affiliation: MIT-IBM Computing Research Lab Polina Golland Affiliation: Massachusetts Institute of Technology Yoon Kim Affiliation: Massachusetts Institute of Technology Zhang-Wei Hong Affiliation: MIT-IBM Computing Research Lab Abstract Self-distillation is an emerging approach that trains a model to imitate its own predictions when conditioned on privileged information, such as correct answers or environmental feedback. Recent methods have reported better sample efficiency and generalization than reinforcement learning (RL), but it remains unclear whether these gains extend beyond in-distribution evaluations and whether they stem from privileged distillation. In this paper, we revisit two representative methods, Self-Distillation Policy Optimization (SDPO) and On-Policy Self-Distillation (OPSD), across code, science, tool use, and math reasoning tasks. We find that their reported gains do not consistently generalize to unseen problems and are largely explained by overfitting or transferring existing reasoning behavior, rather than effectively using privileged information. Moreover, self-distillation gradients show weak alignment with policy gradients that directly optimize expected reward, questioning whether they provide an effective direction for policy improvement. Our experiments across Qwen3 models (1.7B–14B) and OLMo-3-7B suggest that effectively leveraging privileged information to improve reasoning remains an open challenge. Our code is available at https://github.com/sleepypandu/does-sd-improve-reasoning . 1 Introduction Figure 1: SDPO reduces solution diversity on LiveCodeBench v6. AST-unique solutions per training problem (from 8 8 samples) for Qwen3-14B before (Base) and after 200 200 steps of GRPO or SDPO (mean ± \pm std, four seeds). Diversity collapse suggests memorization. Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) has become a standard approach for finetuning large language models (LLMs) on reasoning tasks such as mathematics and coding ( Lambert et al., 2025 ; Guo et al., 2025 ) . As responses in these domains are automatically verifiable, scaling RLVR is straightforward. However, typical RLVR pipelines use binary rewards that indicate only whether the final answer is correct or whether all unit tests pass. Such rewards provide no information on how attempted solutions should be revised. Process reward models score individual reasoning steps ( Wang et al., 2024 ; Cui et al., 2025 ) , but require training additional models. A recently popularized alternative to RLVR is self-distillation, which requires neither an external stronger model nor an explicit reward function. It exploits the fact that reasoning tasks often come with feedback far richer than the binary outcome that RLVR consumes, such as reference solutions, critiques, error messages, or failing unit tests. In self-distillation, the model samples a trace and is trained to match, at each token along that trace, the distribution of a (teacher) copy of itself that additionally conditions on this privileged information ( Hübotter et al., 2026 ; Shenfeld et al., 2026 ; Zhao et al., 2026a ) . The self-conditioned teacher converts rich but unstructured feedback into a dense, per-token training signal. However, prior work questions the validity of self-distillation objectives as a mechanism to improve the student policy. The self-conditioned teacher’s outputs do not express uncertainty through tokens such as “wait”, “hmmm”, and “perhaps,” so training against it suppresses the model’s ability to correct past mistakes as it reasons and shortens the student’s traces in ways that hurt performance ( Kim et al., 2026 ; Pan et al., 2026 ; Kaur et al., 2026 ) . Moreover, the self-conditioned teacher is unable to consistently distinguish between correct and incorrect student generations ( Harne et al., 2026 ; Nguyen et al., 2026 ) . Together, these results raise a more fundamental question: does self-distillation extract useful guidance from privileged information, or are its reported gains driven by simpler mechanisms? Figure 2: The reference solution barely changes the self-distillation teacher. Teacher log-probabilities (thinking on) along a Qwen3-8B thinking-off student rollout on an OpenThoughts problem, with the problem’s reference solution in context (ref z z , top) and with no solution (no z z , bottom). In each panel, the first row is the sampled token and the three rows below are the student’s next three most likely tokens. The two teachers assign very similar probabilities, suggesting that OPSD distillation effects do not significantly depend on z z ( Section 4.3 ). We revisit this question through careful reproductions of self-distillation policy optimization (SDPO) ( Hübotter et al., 2026 ) and on-policy self-distillation (OPSD) ( Zhao et al., 2026a ) . We leverage Qwen3 ( Qwen Team, 2025 ) and Olmo-3 ( Olmo Team, 2025 ) models to ensure our observations hold over a range of model sizes and families. We find that the reported gains from privileged-information self-distillation in reasoning domains are weaker than they appear: • SDPO’s coding gains do not transfer to unseen problems ( Section 4.1 ). SDPO’s LiveCodeBench v6 ( Jain et al., 2024 ) results train and evaluate on the same set of coding problems. Correcting for this reveals that SDPO overfits to its training problems, reducing the diversity of its solutions ( Figure 1 ), and degrades on held-out problems while policy-gradient training on the same verifiable reward generalizes. • SDPO’s gains in science reasoning and tool use do not generalize out of domain ( Section 4.2 ). Across five science-reasoning ( Feng et al., 2024 ) and tool-use ( Tang et al., 2023 ) domains, SDPO and GRPO in-domain performances are similar, but in four of five it lowers accuracy on the held-out domains, while GRPO preserves it. • OPSD transfers thinking-mode behavior, not privileged information ( Section 4.3 ). In math reasoning, privileged information barely changes the self-conditioned teacher’s token distribution ( Figure 2 ), and distilling thinking-mode behavior has a substantially larger effect than privileged information within OPSD’s reported results. • Self-distillation gradients do not align with policy gradients ( Section 4.4 ). Prior work motivates self-distillation as approximate policy improvement by arguing that its gradient aligns with the policy gradient ( Shenfeld et al., 2026 ) . This alignment matters because a small update improves expected reward only to the extent that it follows the policy gradient ( Section 3.1 ). We therefore measure how well each method’s training gradient aligns with the policy gradient on held-out problems. On Qwen3 models, OPSD and SDPO gradients are consistently less aligned than GRPO gradients computed on the same training problems and often point away from the held-out policy gradient altogether. 2 Related works Reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards (RLVR). The goal of reinforcement learning is to maximize the expectation of reward under a policy’s responses. For verifiable tasks in math reasoning and coding, such rewards are assigned by verifying the equivalence between expressions or checking whether a proposed program passes all test-cases. RLVR for LLMs is typically done using policy gradient algorithms ( Williams, 1992 ; Sutton et al., 2000 ) . Although PPO ( Schulman et al., 2017 ) was used in foundational work ( Ouyang et al., 2022 ; Bai et al., 2022 ) , critic-free methods such as GRPO ( Shao et al., 2024 ) and RLOO ( Kool et al., 2019 ; Ahmadian et al., 2024 ) have become popular due to their simplicity, despite feedback only being given at the trajectory level. On-policy distillation (OPD). Unlike classic distillation, which tries to match teacher-generated outputs ( Hinton et al., 2015 ) , on-policy distillation seeks to boost performance by minimizing per-token divergences between student logits π θ ( ⋅ | x , y < t ) \pi_{	heta}(\cdot|x,y_{<t}) and teacher logits π T ( ⋅ | x , y < t ) \pi_{T}(\cdot|x,y_{<t}) along prefixes sampled from the student policy ( Lu and Thinking Machines Lab, 2025 ; Xu et al., 2026 ; Gu et al., 2023 ) . OPD has been shown to be effective in post-training small-models ( Qwen Team, 2025 ) and merging the capabilities of multiple domain-specialist expert models ( Xu et al., 2026 ; Kimi Team, 2026 ; Ma et al., 2026 ) . Self-distillation and privileged information. Instead of relying on an external stronger model, self-distillation ( Shenfeld et al., 2026 ; Zhao et al., 2026a ; Hübotter et al., 2026 ) parameterizes the teacher distribution as π T ( ⋅ | x , y < t ) = π θ ( ⋅ | x , z , y < t ) \pi_{T}(\cdot|x,y_{<t})=\pi_{	heta}(\cdot|x,z,y_{<t}) , with privileged information z z which is derived from a reference solution or environmental feedback. This purportedly moves the student towards a higher-reward policy, transferring the reference solution’s reasoning content. Limitations of self-distillation. Prior work documents several failure modes of self-distillation: SDPO generalizes poorly out of distribution and in continual learning settings ( Wang et al., 2026 ) , self-distillation degrades thinking models by suppressing backtracking behavior and collapsing response lengths ( Kaur et al., 2026 ; Harne et al., 2026 ) , and the self-conditioned teacher fails to distinguish correct from incorrect student solutions ( Nguyen et al., 2026 ) . Concurrently, Shrestha and Tessier (2026) show that OPSD’s gains stem from thinking-mode transfer rather than privileged information. Methods that retain GRPO and use the teacher only to reweight advantages such as Pan et al. (2026) fall outside our scope, as we study the self-distillation objective in isolation. We provide an extended discussion of existing work on the limitations of self-distillation, and of how our paper differs from each, in Appendix A . 3 Preliminaries We introduce the baseline training objectives, then show in Section 3.1 that under an optimal teacher all three share the same update direction, which motivates the gradient analysis of Section 4.4 . Notation. Let 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , 𝒴 \mathcal{Y} , and 𝒵 \mathcal{Z} denote the spaces of problems, solutions, and privileged information respectively. We assume all three are finite, as is the case for language models with a finite vocabulary and a maximum sequence length. For y ∈ 𝒴 y\in\mathcal{Y} , we write y t y_{t} for its t t -th token, y < t y_{<t} for its first t − 1 t-1 tokens, and | y | |y| for its length. For distributions p , q p,q over 𝒴 \mathcal{Y} , D KL ( 1 ∥ 2 ) p q = ∑ y ∈ 𝒴 p ( y ) log p ⁡ ( y ) q ⁡ ( y ) D_{\mathrm{KL}}(#1\,\|\,#2){p}{q}=\sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}p(y)\log rac{p(y)}{q(y)} . Supervised fine-tuning (SFT). Given a distribution over problems x ∼ ρ ⁡ ( ⋅ ) x\simho(\cdot) and a distribution over solutions y ∼ π T ( ⋅ | x ) y\sim\pi_{T}(\cdot|x) , supervised finetuning optimizes π θ ( ⋅ | x ) \pi_{	heta}(\cdot|x) to maximize the likelihood of y y : ℒ SFT ​ ( θ ) = − 𝔼 x ∼ ρ ⁡ ( ⋅ ) , y ∼ π T ​ ( y | x ) ​ [ ∑ t = 1 | y | log ⁡ π θ ​ ( y t ∣ x , y < t ) ] . \mathcal{L}_{\operatorname{SFT}}(	heta)=-\mathbb{E}_{x\simho(\cdot),y\sim\p