). train_seen and train_unseen evaluate training problems on seen and on held-out test cases respectively; test_unseen evaluates held-out problems. The 20000 20000 training generations reported by Hübotter et al. (2026) is marked in red. Setup. LiveCodeBench v6 pairs each competitive programming problem with a set of test cases. Concretely, the original work trains on all problems with half of each problem’s test cases removed, and evaluates on the unmodified dataset. To separate memorization from generalization, we instead split the problems into disjoint, equal-size sets 𝒫 train \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{train}} and 𝒫 test \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{test}} , giving three evaluation groups: train_seen (test cases used during training), train_unseen (held-out test cases of problems in 𝒫 train \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{train}} ), and test_unseen (all test cases of problems in 𝒫 test \mathcal{P}_{\mathrm{test}} ). Results. We train Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-14B, and Olmo-3-7B-Instruct on this split with SDPO and GRPO for 200 steps, four seeds each, otherwise following the setup of Hübotter et al. (2026) ; we provide our hyperparameters in Section E.1 . Figure 3 shows Qwen3-8B; results for the other two models and tables of accuracies are in Section D.1 . SDPO does not improve on new problems. Over 200 steps, GRPO improves test_unseen by 3.7 3.7 , 4.9 4.9 , and 2.6 2.6 points on Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-14B, and Olmo-3-7B-Instruct, whereas SDPO changes it by − 2.5 -2.5 , − 0.5 -0.5 , and − 9.9 -9.9 points. The disparity is largest on Olmo-3-7B-Instruct, where SDPO ends 13.1 13.1 points below GRPO ( 21.0 21.0 vs. 34.1 34.1 ). In summary, we find that mimicking a privileged teacher does not generalize to new problems. SDPO does generalize to held-out test cases. SDPO’s gains on train_seen largely carry over to train_unseen ( + 23.1 +23.1 vs. + 18.4 +18.4 on Qwen3-8B, + 22.5 +22.5 vs. + 17.2 +17.2 on Qwen3-14B, and + 18.3 +18.3 vs. + 16.7 +16.7 on Olmo-3-7B-Instruct), retaining 76–91% of the gain compared 